  "learning_objective": "Explain how a load balancer spreads incoming requests across multiple servers so no single server gets overwhelmed.",
  "audience": "A beginner who uses websites daily and knows that servers exist but has never thought about how one site handles many visitors at once.",
  "prerequisites": [],
  "brief": {
    "learner": "A beginner who uses websites daily and knows that servers exist but has never thought about how one site handles many visitors at once.",
    "can_do": "Explain how a load balancer spreads incoming requests across multiple servers so no single server gets overwhelmed.",
    "question": "How does one website handle thousands of visitors at once?",
    "problem": "A small online store launches a flash sale and thousands of people click the site in the first minute; the single server running the site slows to a crawl and starts returning errors.",
    "naive_attempt": "A beginner expects the one server to simply get faster or that the web traffic will somehow sort itself out, but a single server has fixed capacity and all requests pile onto its one CPU and network card.",
    "turning_point": "A load balancer sits in front of several identical servers and hands each new request to whichever server is free, so the work is shared.",
    "check_question": "What does the load balancer do when a server is busy?",
    "check_answer": "It sends the next request to a different, available server instead."
  },
